> Probably better to zap them with cancellation then polypile so they
> have +0 enchantment and kill the cursed status.

If you're polymorphing strictly for magical armor/tools, BUC status
almost doesn't matter. Cursed status (and pile size) affect the
probability of polyfodder vanishing. However, the probability a given
piece of magical armor/tool will instead become non-magical /and stay
so) is so much larger that you barely increase your yield by uncursing
stuff.

If you're happy with non-magical armor or tools but have limited
fodder, it's well worth uncursing and even blessing it. The confused
blessed scroll of remove curse effect is probably the most efficient
way to mass bless stuff. (Lift the resulting curses with skilled or
blessed remove curse, the prayer boon, or cancellation.) However, junk
isn't usually scare. Mass blessing probably only makes sense if you
are bent on getting magical armor out of Fort Ludios.

Enchantment and charges have no effect on polypiling. They are simply
preserved (except for rings). The only practical implication is you
can enchant elven armor and a wizard's cornuthaum more than other
armor; so if you really want +7 armor, enchant it before polypiling.

If you care enough about polypile yield to uncurse magical items, you
are most likely overpreparing or attempting some very demanding
conduct.

All the gory detail is available at http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Polypiling#Strategy
